//! lotus/stereo_expand_env_hold.rye — ALES168: the ATTACK/HOLD/RELEASE DOWNWARD EXPANDER carried into stereo — the LINKED HELD envelope (ALES165) driving ONE LINKED widening (ALES159) over time, the third spender of the held
//! time base. ALES164 carried the breathing expander into stereo: one shared envelope deepening a pair as it falls away and easing off as it rises, one widened gain applied to both channels so the image never lurches. Yet an
//! un-held expander still deepens and re-opens on a brief DIP between two loud samples — it pumps, and the ear hears the gain move across the whole field at once. A real expander carries a HOLD knob for exactly this: on a fall
//! away from a peak, it pins the peak for a little while before the release begins, so the widening stays idle through the dip and the passage stays whole. ALES165 built that hold stage INSIDE the linked follower and proved it
//! once. This rung spends that proof: it drives ALES60's deepened floor from the LINKED HELD envelope rather than the plain linked one — the same stereo expander, now with the patience that keeps it from pumping the image.
//!
//! Two proofs fused, held. The DETECTOR is the linked HELD follower (ALES165): one shared envelope steps toward the linked peak max(|left|, |right|), the ONE shared hold countdown pinning that peak across a fall so the two
//! channels never re-open on different samples. The GAIN is linked (ALES159): where the held envelope falls below the threshold its deficit is widened by the ratio (ALES60's deepened floor, exp = max(0, threshold − deficit·num/den),
//! computed on the ENVELOPE not the bare sample) and the single gain exp/e is applied to BOTH raw samples. The gain math is not re-invented (it is ALES60's), and the time base is not re-invented (it is ALES165's linked held
//! env_step_hold); this rung is exactly their composition over public APIs, adding only the hold knob to the stereo breathing expander. It is the stereo mirror of ALES62's held mono expander.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadThreshold (outside [1, sample_max]), BadRatio (below one or a zero denominator), BadCoeff (an attack or release outside (0, 1]), BadHold (a hold longer than the widest clip), and BadRange
//! (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any write. Otherwise walk the span one sample-PAIR at a time, advancing ONE i64 envelope with ONE u32 hold countdown toward the linked peak by ALES165's stereo_env_step_hold:
//! envelope at or above the threshold leaves the pair playing (both pass byte for byte, continuous at the threshold); envelope below the threshold applies the one widened gain exp/e to both raw samples. A shared held envelope
//! of exactly zero arises only when both channels are silent this pair, so nothing to expand and no divide by zero.
//!
//! The crux — six laws. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW (the crux link): a hold of zero makes the linked held follower the plain linked follower, so this equals ALES164's stereo_expand_env byte for byte on both — the held rung's no-hold
//! limit is exactly the un-held rung already proven. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' equal to ALES62's mono expand_env_hold(left) byte for byte, the right scaled by the same fraction exp/e,
//! the independent per-channel expand_env_hold genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase, no magnitude expanding, no sign flipping. THE KNOB /
//! NO-PUMP LAWS: unit ratio the identity everywhere, and a NONZERO HOLD keeping the expander idle across a brief dip (it does not pump, the held magnitude no smaller than the un-held one and strictly louder at the dip).
//! THE CARRY LAW: a span expanded in two pieces (the split mid-hold) equals the whole once on both, env AND hold_left carrying. THE AT-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked held
//! envelope never falls below the threshold passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES168.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-073427_fill-ales168-lotus-stereo-expand-env-hold.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_hold_env.rye (ALES165) — the LINKED HELD follower, its stereo_env_step_hold advancing the one shared envelope with the one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak, reused whole;
//! lotus/stereo_expand_env.rye (ALES164) — the un-held linked breathing expander this reduces to at a hold of zero, re-proven beside it as the zero-hold limit; lotus/expand_env_hold.rye (ALES62) — the mono attack/hold/release
//! expander, its ExpandEnvHoldError reused whole and its output the left-always-peak comparison; lotus/expand.rye (ALES60) — the deepened floor exp = max(0, threshold − deficit·num/den); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_max, max_clip.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared u32 hold countdown, siloed to lotus/. One linked-peak comparison and at most one
//! widen and one multiply-then-divide for the envelope step plus one per channel for the gain, all in i64 (|sample|·exp <= 32768·32767 never overflowing), the gain exp/e in [0, 1) so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's
//! and fits i16. The threshold is a magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain fraction, the attack, release, and hold counted in sample INDICES rather than milliseconds against a clock. No real sample rate, no anti-aliasing,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_expand_env_hold.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const stereo_hold_env = @import("stereo_hold_env.rye");
const stereo_expand_env = @import("stereo_expand_env.rye");
const expand_env_hold = @import("expand_env_hold.rye");
const expand = @import("expand.rye");

pub const stereo_expand_env_hold_version = "20260815.073427";

/// The linked attack/hold/release expander's faults are exactly ALES62's own `ExpandEnvHoldError` — `BadThreshold`, `BadRatio`, `BadCoeff`, `BadHold`, `BadRange` — reused by name, since fusing the linked held detector with the
/// linked widening adds no new fault: it validates one shared threshold, one shared ratio, one shared attack, one shared release, one shared hold, and one span, then advances a single held envelope and applies one gain per pair
/// to both channels together.
pub const StereoExpandEnvHoldError = expand_env_hold.ExpandEnvHoldError;

/// Expand `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc` below the magnitude `threshold` by the ratio `ratio_num/ratio_den`, the ONE downward widening FOLLOWING the LINKED HELD envelope that CONTINUES from the
/// caller's shared `env` and `hold_left` rather than from silence — the carried-state form (ALES43's lesson), now carrying BOTH the shared envelope and the shared hold countdown so the hold survives across a call boundary.
/// Each pair advances ALES165's linked held follower one step toward the linked peak `max(|left|, |right|)` (attack while rising and rearming the shared hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, release once it expires);
/// where the held envelope sits at or above the threshold the expander is idle and both channels pass byte for byte, and where it falls below, the envelope's deficit is widened by the ratio (`exp = max(0, threshold − deficit·num/den)`,
/// ALES60's deepened floor on the linked held envelope) and the single gain `exp/e` applied to BOTH raw samples — one detector, one hold, one widening, the image held. Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, and
/// BadRange before any write; on refusal `env`, `hold_left`, and both channels are untouched. Values only — never the length, and the channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_expand_hold_follow_carry(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    hold_left: *u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) StereoExpandEnvHoldError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the threshold is a legal magnitude — at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (threshold < 1) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (threshold > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    // invariant: the ratio is at least one (num >= den) with a nonzero denominator — a ratio below one would narrow the deficit (an upward gesture), a zero denominator would divide by zero. An expander only ever widens.
    if (ratio_den == 0) return error.BadRatio;
    if (ratio_num < ratio_den) return error.BadRatio;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] — checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the hold is bounded by the widest clip — a longer hold could never expire within any span. Before any write.
    if (hold_samples > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length — checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never expands one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const thr: i64 = @as(i64, threshold);
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_den);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the ONE shared linked HELD follower toward the pair's linked peak (ALES165's proven step); e is the smoothed-and-held linked magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = stereo_hold_env.stereo_env_step_hold(env, hold_left, xl, xr, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the linked held envelope stays a legal magnitude — never negative, never past the rail (the held step's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e < thr) {
            if (e == 0) {
                // The held linked detector reads exact silence. stereo_env_step_hold forces at least a unit step toward any nonzero linked peak and the hold only ever PINS a peak (it never lowers the envelope), so a zero
                // envelope can only arise when this pair's linked peak is zero — both channels are silent this sample, nothing to expand, and no divide by zero.
                // invariant: the tail is fully attenuated when this runs, which is what the
                // envelope exists to reach -- a residue here means the release never completed
                // and the next clip would open over it
                assert(xl == 0 and xr == 0);
            } else {
                // The expanded envelope target — ALES60's deepened floor computed on the LINKED HELD envelope, not the bare sample: the deficit thr − e widened by the ratio (num/den >= 1) and floored at zero. thr−e <= 32767.
                const deficit: i64 = thr - e; // strictly positive here (e < thr)
                const widened: i64 = @divTrunc(deficit * num, den);
                const raw_exp: i64 = thr - widened;
                const exp: i64 = if (raw_exp < 0) 0 else raw_exp; // floor at silence — the gate reached from within the expander
                // invariant: the expanded envelope sits within [0, envelope] — the single gain exp/e is in [0, 1) (num/den >= 1 => exp <= e).
                assert(exp >= 0 and exp <= e);
                const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
                const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
                // ONE shared gain exp/e across both channels: m·exp <= 32768·32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps each quotient at most its m.
                const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * exp, e);
                const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * exp, e);
                // invariant: the shared gain reduces, never boosts, and never below zero, on each channel — a smoothed expander only ever leans down.
                assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nr <= mr and nr >= 0);
                const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ml <= sample_max
                const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
                sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
                sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
                // invariant: each written sample holds its input's sign (magnitude only shrank), zero being sign-neutral.
                assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
                assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
            }
        }
        // held envelope at or above the threshold: the pair plays — both left exactly as they were (continuous at the threshold); the hold keeping the pinned peak above the threshold is what keeps the expander idle through a
        // brief dip rather than pumping.
    }

    // invariant: an expander changes sample values only — each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

/// Expand `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc`, the one downward widening following a LINKED HELD envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh shared env of 0 with the hold unarmed) — one
/// implementation shared with the carried form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn stereo_expand_hold_follow(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) StereoExpandEnvHoldError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    var hold_left: u32 = 0;
    return stereo_expand_hold_follow_carry(sc, start, count, &env, &hold_left, threshold, ratio_num, ratio_den, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
}

// --- selftest: THE ZERO-HOLD LAW — a hold of zero reduces to ALES164's stereo_expand_env byte for byte on both; THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW — a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES62's mono expand_env_hold(left), the
//     right scaled by the same fraction, the independent push differing; THE IMAGE LAW — no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase; THE KNOB / NO-PUMP LAWS — unit ratio the identity,
//     a nonzero hold keeping the expander idle across a dip; THE CARRY LAW — a span in two pieces (split mid-hold) equals the whole once on both; THE AT-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW --------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW — the crux link. With hold_samples == 0 the linked held follower's fall branch never pins, so every pair moves through ALES165's step exactly as ALES164's un-held stereo_expand_env does: the held
    //    stereo expander with no hold IS the ALES164 stereo expander, byte-for-byte by construction. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same master and demanding equality on both channels (the no-hold limit).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 800, -800, 5000, -600, 200, -9000, 400, -400 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 300, -1200, 500, -50, 12000, -250, 250 };
        var held = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&held, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&held, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 0); // hold of zero, real attack/release
        var plain = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&plain, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_env.stereo_expand_follow(&plain, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5); // the proven ALES164 un-held linked expander
        if (!eq(held.left.samples(), plain.left.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldLeftNotPlain;
        if (!eq(held.right.samples(), plain.right.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldRightNotPlain;
    }

    // 2. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW — the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere) makes the shared held envelope equal the mono held envelope of the left, so left' equals
    //    ALES62's mono expand_env_hold(left) byte for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction exp/e — a different, image-preserving result than expanding the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 12000, 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 6000, -6000 }; // the left holds the peak; a loud head, then a quiet tail, then loud again
        const rr = [_]i16{ -3000, 50, -40, 30, -50, 40, -1500, 1500 }; // |right| <= |left| everywhere
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2); // instant attack, release 1:2, hold 2

        // the peak-holding left equals ALES62's mono expand_env_hold of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try expand_env_hold.expand_hold_follow(&mono_l, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;

        // the INDEPENDENT push genuinely differs — mono expand_env_hold of the right alone follows the right's own (quieter) held envelope, an image-shifting result.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try expand_env_hold.expand_hold_follow(&mono_r, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2);
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW — the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase pair (right = −left) comes back STILL out of phase, both
    //    scaled by the identical time-smoothed-and-held fraction.
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 5000, -400, 300, -300 };
        const ir = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -3000, 200, -250, 250 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 6) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }

        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 600, -800, 400, -200 }, &[_]i16{ -600, 800, -400, 200 });
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&op, 0, 4, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
    }

    // 4a. UNIT RATIO IS THE IDENTITY EVERYWHERE on both — ratio_num == ratio_den makes exp == e, so the gain is exactly one at every sample regardless of smoothing or hold: the whole master passes byte for byte, even below threshold.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 500, -900, 20, -400 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -700, 200, -50, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 3, 3, 1, 4, 1, 8, 4); // unit ratio 3:3, real attack/release, hold 4
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.UnitRatioNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4b. A NONZERO HOLD KEEPS THE EXPANDER IDLE ACROSS A DIP — a loud pair, one quiet dip below the threshold, then loud again, the left holding the linked peak. With INSTANT attack and release and NO hold the dip drops the
    //     envelope below the threshold and is deepened (the expander pumped). With a hold that spans the dip the pinned peak stays above the threshold so the expander stays idle and the dip passes byte-for-byte. So the held
    //     magnitude is no SMALLER than the un-held one at every sample (the hold only keeps the expander IDLE, monotone), and strictly LOUDER at the dip (the pumping avoided).
    {
        const dl = [_]i16{ 15000, 300, 15000 }; // loud, a quiet dip below threshold, loud again — the left holds the linked peak
        const dr = [_]i16{ -400, 50, -400 };
        const thr: i32 = 1000;
        var none = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&none, &dl, &dr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&none, 0, 3, thr, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0); // instant attack, instant release, no hold
        var held = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&held, &dl, &dr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&held, 0, 3, thr, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1); // same, hold of 1 spans the single dip sample
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 3) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(held.left.samples()[i]) < abs16(none.left.samples()[i])) return error.HoldExpandedMore;
        }
        // the dip sample (index 1): the held expander reads the pinned peak above threshold and lets it pass, the un-held expander has already dropped below threshold and deepens it — the expander held idle through the dip.
        if (abs16(held.left.samples()[1]) <= abs16(none.left.samples()[1])) return error.HoldDidNotSurvive;
    }

    // 5. THE CARRY LAW — the shared state carries, env AND hold_left both — so expanding a span in two pieces (the split falling MID-HOLD, the second continuing the first's ending shared envelope and countdown) equals expanding
    //    the whole span once, byte for byte on BOTH channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 12000, 12000, 400, 400, 300, 300, 9000, 200 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -3000, 2000, -100, 90, -80, 70, -1500, 50 };
        var whole = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&whole, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&whole, 0, 8, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2);

        var split = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&split, &ll, &rr);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        var hold_left: u32 = 0;
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2); // first piece — split at index 3 mid-hold
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow_carry(&split, 3, 5, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2); // second continues env AND hold_left
        if (!eq(split.left.samples(), whole.left.samples())) return error.CarryLeftNotEqual;
        if (!eq(split.right.samples(), whole.right.samples())) return error.CarryRightNotEqual;
    }

    // 6a. THE AT-THRESHOLD LAW — a master whose linked held envelope never falls below the threshold passes byte for byte on both (the expander never opens).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 5000, -9000, 1200, -1000, 8000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ 3000, -80, 40, -1000, -50 }; // even where the right is quiet the left is loud, so the linked envelope stays at or above threshold
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 8, 3); // instant attack seats the loud head, the envelope never dips below 1000
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.AtThresholdNotIdentity;
    }

    // 6b. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — an all-silent master expands to zero on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 500, -800, 200, -900, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 300, -700, 500, -50 });
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&bc, 0, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 6c. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a bad threshold, a sub-unity ratio, an illegal coefficient, a hold past max_clip, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand_hold_follow(&a, 0, 3, 0, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ThresholdRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand_hold_follow(&b, 0, 3, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.SubUnityRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand_hold_follow(&c, 0, 3, 1000, 4, 1, 5, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.AttackRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand_hold_follow(&d, 0, 3, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.HoldRefusalMutated;

        var f = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&f, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_expand_hold_follow(&f, 1, 3, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(f.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(f.right.samples(), &tr) or f.left.len != f.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 6d. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to expand.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 200 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_expand_hold_follow(&noop, 1, 0, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 300, -300, 200 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    // silence the unused import in builds that trim it — expand is named in the doc lineage; touch it so the reference stays honest.
    _ = expand.expand_version;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-expand-env-hold: the ATTACK/HOLD/RELEASE DOWNWARD EXPANDER CARRIED INTO STEREO — the LINKED HELD envelope driving ONE LINKED widening over time, the third spender of the held time base. Expand a " ++
        "span of sample-PAIRS in a master below a magnitude threshold by a ratio, the one downward widening following the shared LINKED HELD envelope (max(|left|, |right|), attack while rising and rearming the shared hold, the " ++
        "peak pinned while the countdown lasts, release once it expires): at or above the threshold both pass byte for byte (continuous at the threshold), below it the deepened floor exp = max(0, threshold − deficit·num/den) " ++
        "(ALES60's floor on the linked held envelope) yields one gain exp/e applied to both raw samples. ALES164 built the un-held stereo breathing expander; ALES165 built the linked held time base; this fuses them — one " ++
        "detector, one hold, one widening, the image held the whole time, the hold keeping the expander idle across a dip rather than pumping the field. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW (the crux link): a hold of zero reduces to ALES164's " ++
        "stereo_expand_env byte for byte on both. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES62's mono expand_env_hold(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent per-channel " ++
        "expand_env_hold genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE KNOB / NO-PUMP LAWS: unit ratio " ++
        "the identity everywhere, a nonzero hold keeping the expander idle across a brief dip (it does not pump, the knob monotone). THE CARRY LAW: a span expanded in two pieces (the split mid-hold) equals the whole once on " ++
        "both, env and hold_left carrying. THE AT-THRESHOLD / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked held envelope never falls below the threshold passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, " ++
        "left.len == right.len after, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared " ++
        "u32 hold countdown, at most one multiply-then-divide for the step plus one per channel for the gain (in i64, no overflow), the gain exp/e in [0, 1); the attack, release, and hold per sample index; no real sample rate, " ++
        "no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
